  "question": {
    "text": "Gives the line \\(L: \\frac{x-1}{3}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}\\) and the plane \\(\\pi: x-2 y-z=0\\). Of the following assertions, the only one that is always true is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "iAHrn",
      "text": "L is \\(\\perp\\) to \\(\\pi\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bg6Fc",
      "text": "L lies in \\(\\pi\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Hm4A6",
      "text": "L is not parallel to \\(\\pi\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "tKlKO",
      "text": "None of these",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since \\(3(1)+2(-2)+(-1)(-1)=3-4+1=0\\)\n\\(\\therefore\\) Given line is \\(\\perp\\) to the normal to the plane i.e., given line is parallel to the given plane.\nAlso, \\((1,-1,3)\\) lies on the plane \\(x-2 y-z=0\\) if \\(1-2(-1)-3=0\\) i.e., \\(1+2-3=0\\) which is true \\(\\therefore\\) L lies in plane \\(\\pi\\).",
    "image": null
  },
